Practical No. 2 
 
Aim: Process Creation & Termination – Implement a C program to create child processes using fork() and manage termination with wait(). 
 
Theory: 
1. Process:- 
A process is a program that is currently running. 

2.	What is Process Creation? 
Process creation means creating a new process from an existing process. 
In Linux/Unix, this is done using the fork() system call. 
•	The original process is called the Parent Process.  
•	The new process is called the Child Process
 
3.	What is fork()? 
fork() is a system call that creates a new child process by copying the parent process. 
Return values: 
•	0 → Child process  
•	Positive value → Parent process (child PID)  
•	-1 → Error   
4.	Parent Process 
•	Creates the child process.  
•	Can wait for the child using wait().  
5.	Child Process 
•	Executes independently after fork().  
•	Has its own Process ID (PID).   
6.	wait() 
•	Makes the parent process wait until the child finishes.  
•	Header file:  #include <sys/wait.h> 
Syntax: wait(NULL); 
 
7.	Why use wait()? 
•	Synchronizes parent and child.  
•	Prevents zombie processes.  
 
8.	Process Termination 
A process ends after completing its execution or by using return 0; or exit(). 
 
Here's a simple C program for Process Creation & Termination using fork() and wait(). 

Code:
#include <stdio.h> 
#include <unistd.h> 
#include <sys/wait.h> 
 
int main() 
{    // Declares a variable pid      pid_t pid; 
 
    // Calls the fork() system call and Create a child process      pid = fork(); 
     if (pid < 0) 
    { 
        printf("Process creation failed!\n"); 
    } 
    else if (pid == 0) 
    { 
        // Child process 
        printf("Child Process\n"); 
        printf("Child PID = %d\n", getpid()); 
    }     else 
    { 
        // Parent process waits for child         wait(NULL);         printf("Parent Process\n");         printf("Parent PID = %d\n", getpid()); 
    } 
 
    return 0; 
} 
 
Output:
Child Process 
Child PID = 2456 
Parent Process 
Parent PID = 2455 
 
